# Playwright Conventions

Full standard for specs under `tests/**`. Prefer patterns already in this repo; where a section has no in-repo example yet, the ✅ block is **advised**.

## 1. Locators

**Standard:** Role/label first; `getByTestId` escape hatch only (comment why); never CSS/XPath; disambiguate with `.filter({ hasText })`, not `.first()`.

```typescript
// ✅ Good — from pages/ProgramsPage.ts
this.newProgramButton = page.getByRole("button", { name: "+ New Program" });
return this.programsTable
  .getByRole("row")
  .filter({ has: this.page.getByText(name, { exact: true }) });
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
page.locator(".btn-primary");
page.locator("//button[1]");
page.getByRole("button", { name: /^Edit / }).first();
```

**Why:** Prevents brittle DOM coupling and non-deterministic matches when multiple similar controls exist.

## 2. Waiting

**Standard:** Web-first assertions only; never `waitForTimeout`; never snapshot booleans from `isVisible()`.

```typescript
// ✅ Good — from tests/ds1-create-program.spec.ts
await expect(programsPage.heading).toBeVisible();
await expect(modal.dialog).toBeVisible();
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
await page.waitForTimeout(2000);
expect(await modal.dialog.isVisible()).toBe(true);
```

**Why:** Prevents race flake from fixed sleeps and one-shot visibility checks that do not retry.

## 3. Page Objects

**Standard:** One POM per page/component; locators in the constructor; no assertions in POMs; specs never inline locators.

```typescript
// ✅ Good — from pages/NewProgramModal.ts + ds1 spec
async fillProgramName(name: string): Promise<void> {
  await this.programName.fill(name);
}
// in spec:
await programsPage.openNewProgramForm();
await expect(modal.dialog).toBeVisible();
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
async openForm(): Promise<void> {
  await this.newProgramButton.click();
  await expect(this.dialog).toBeVisible(); // assertion in POM
}
// in spec:
await page.getByRole("button", { name: "+ New Program" }).click();
```

**Why:** Prevents duplicated locators and mixed intent/mechanics that break every screen when the UI shifts.

## 4. Auth

**Standard:** `storageState` via the setup project for feature tests; empty session + UI login only for login-page tests; API login for speed / programmatic setup.

```typescript
// ✅ Good — playwright.config.ts setup + storageState dependency
{ name: "setup", testMatch: /.*\.setup\.ts/ },
{
  name: "chromium",
  use: { ...devices["Desktop Chrome"], storageState: "playwright/.auth/user.json" },
  dependencies: ["setup"],
}
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad — logging in via UI at the start of every feature test
test.beforeEach(async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto("/login");
  await page.getByLabel("Email").fill(process.env.DIDAXIS_EMAIL!);
  await page.getByRole("button", { name: "Sign In" }).click();
});
```

**Why:** Prevents slow, flaky per-test login and session drift across the suite. See `auth-strategy.md`.

## 5. Tags

**Standard:** Tag each test with **exactly one** of `@smoke`, `@sanity`, `@regression`, `@api`, `@e2e`, or `@destructive` on the `test()` call — never on `describe`.

| Tag | When to use |
|-----|-------------|
| `@smoke` | Fast critical-path checks after deploy (login, one happy create, key nav) |
| `@sanity` | Core product flows broader than smoke but still short |
| `@regression` | Full feature coverage — validation, a11y, edge cases, network mocks |
| `@api` | Contract or helper checks via Playwright `request` (no browser journey) |
| `@e2e` | Multi-page UI journeys that exercise real navigation end-to-end |
| `@destructive` | **Only** when the test mutates **shared/global** state (locale, roles, feature flags, org settings) |

**`@destructive` rules:**

- A test that creates data and cleans up **only its own** records via `trackProgram` keeps its importance tag (`@smoke`, `@regression`, etc.) — **not** `@destructive`.
- Any test that changes shared/global state **must** register an `afterEach` or `afterAll` hook that reverts the mutation before the test file finishes.
- Run destructive tests serially: `npm run test:destructive` (uses `--workers=1`). Do not set `workers: 1` globally in config.

```typescript
// ✅ Good — importance tag; self-cleaning data only
test(
  "TC-002 — Program is created successfully",
  { tag: "@smoke" },
  async ({ page, trackProgram }) => {
    const { name, description } = buildProgram();
    await programsPage.createProgram(name, trackProgram, description);
    await expect(programsPage.programRow(name)).toBeVisible();
  },
);

// ✅ Good — shared state mutation + mandatory revert hook
test.afterEach(async ({ page }) => {
  await settingsPage.restoreDefaultLocale();
});

test(
  "TC-050 — Admin switches org locale to French",
  { tag: "@destructive" },
  async ({ page }) => {
    await settingsPage.setOrgLocale("fr-CA");
    await expect(settingsPage.localeDisplay).toHaveText("fr-CA");
  },
);
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad — multiple tags
test("…", { tag: ["@smoke", "@e2e"] }, async () => {});

// ❌ Bad — @destructive for data the test owns and deletes via trackProgram
test("create program", { tag: "@destructive" }, async ({ trackProgram }) => {});

// ❌ Bad — mutates org locale with no revert hook
test("switch locale", { tag: "@destructive" }, async () => {
  await settingsPage.setOrgLocale("de-DE");
});

// ❌ Bad — tag on describe
test.describe("Programs", { tag: "@regression" }, () => {});
```

**Why:** Tags drive CI slices (`npm run test:smoke`, etc.) and keep parallel runs safe — destructive/global mutations must not race other workers.

**Run tagged slices:**

```bash
npm run test:smoke
npm run test:sanity
npm run test:regression
npm run test:api
npm run test:e2e
npm run test:destructive   # --workers=1
```

## 6. Test data

**Standard:** Faker factories under `test-data/factories/` for happy-path payloads; curated invalid sets in static `.ts` files under `test-data/invalid/`; secrets and base URLs from `process.env`; repeated API paths and UI copy from enums under `test-data/enums/`. Every test cleans up data it creates via `trackProgram` (or equivalent teardown).

```typescript
// ✅ Good — factory + cleanup fixture (test-data/factories/program.factory.ts)
import { buildProgram } from "../test-data/factories/program.factory";
import { INVALID_PROGRAM_NAMES } from "../test-data/invalid/program-names";
import { ApiRoutes } from "../test-data/enums/api-routes";

const { name, description } = buildProgram();
await programsPage.createProgram(name, trackProgram, description);

await modal.fillProgramName(INVALID_PROGRAM_NAMES.whitespaceOnly);
await expect(modal.createButton).toBeDisabled();

const res = await request.get(ApiRoutes.Programs, {
  headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.DIDAXIS_API_TOKEN}` },
});
```

```typescript
// ✅ Good — example factory (test-data/factories/program.factory.ts)
import { faker } from "@faker-js/faker";

export function buildProgram(overrides: Partial<ProgramInput> = {}): ProgramInput {
  const suffix = Date.now();
  return {
    name: overrides.name ?? `${faker.commerce.productName()} ${suffix}`,
    description: overrides.description ?? faker.lorem.sentence(),
  };
}
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
const programName = "Web Development 2026"; // fixed name, collisions
const token = "Bearer eyJhbGciOi..."; // hardcoded secret
const invalidName = faker.lorem.word(); // random invalid input — use curated set instead
```

**Why:** Prevents cross-test collisions, unrealistic edge inputs, duplicated route strings, and leaked credentials.

## 7. API testing

**Standard:** Prefer Playwright `request` for setup, teardown helpers, and contract checks; never hardcode tokens; never delete data the test did not create. *(Program teardown today uses `fixtures/cleanup.fixture.ts` — keep that path for UI-created programs.)*

```typescript
// ✅ Good — advised (no `request`-based contract check in repo yet)
test("GET /api/programs returns an array", async ({ request }) => {
  const res = await request.get("/api/programs", {
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.DIDAXIS_API_TOKEN}` },
  });
  expect(res.ok()).toBeTruthy();
  expect(Array.isArray(await res.json())).toBeTruthy();
});
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
const res = await fetch("https://hardcoded-host/api/programs", {
  headers: { Authorization: "Bearer eyJhbGciOi..." }, // hardcoded token
});
```

**Why:** Prevents environment lock-in, leaked secrets, and teardown that destroys shared or foreign data.

## 8. Network mocking

**Standard:** `page.route` / `fulfill` (or abort) for **500** / **503** / **timeout** / empty / malformed; never mock the endpoint under test; observe real UI copy first; one tag per test.

```typescript
// ✅ Good — advised shape (repo has abort-style TC-012; prefer fulfill + narrow URL)
await page.route("**/api/programs**", async (route) => {
  if (route.request().method() === "POST") {
    return route.fulfill({ status: 503, body: '{"error":"unavailable"}' });
  }
  return route.continue();
});
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
await page.route("**/*", (route) => route.abort()); // too broad; blinds the whole app
// inventing toast text the app never shows
await expect(page.getByText("Service Unavailable!!!")).toBeVisible();
```

**Why:** Prevents non-deterministic live-API edge flakes and false failures from invented copy or over-broad routes. See `.cursor/skills/network-mocked-edge-cases/`.

## 9. Accessibility

**Standard:** Axe with `.withTags(['wcag2a','wcag2aa'])` on key pages + a keyboard-nav check; never `.disableRules()` to go green.

```typescript
// ✅ Good — advised (programs.a11y.spec.ts exists but lacks withTags / keyboard / still uses disableRules)
const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page })
  .withTags(["wcag2a", "wcag2aa"])
  .analyze();
await expect(results.violations).toEqual([]);

await programsPage.newProgramButton.focus();
await expect(programsPage.newProgramButton).toBeFocused();
await page.keyboard.press("Enter");
await expect(programsPage.newProgramModal.dialog).toBeVisible();
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad — from current modal a11y pattern to avoid going forward
const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page })
  .disableRules(["color-contrast"]) // silences real issues
  .analyze();
```

**Why:** Prevents shipping inaccessible UI and false-green a11y suites. See `.cursor/skills/a11y-checks/`.

## 10. Assertions

**Standard:** Web-first expects; `expect.soft` for independent multi-checks; visual (`toHaveScreenshot`) only where it earns it.

```typescript
// ✅ Good — soft multi-check advised (repo uses hard expects in ds1 TC-001 today)
await expect.soft(modal.programName).toBeVisible();
await expect.soft(modal.description).toBeVisible();
await expect.soft(modal.createButton).toBeDisabled();
// Visual only when intentional:
// await expect(programsPage.heading).toHaveScreenshot("programs-heading.png");
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
await expect(modal.programName).toBeVisible();
await expect(modal.description).toBeVisible(); // hard stop hides later failures
await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot(); // blanket visual by default
```

**Why:** Prevents masked secondary failures and brittle pixel diffs on layouts that are not under visual contract.

## 11. Anti-flake

**Standard:** CI-only retries (max 2); isolation; `page.clock` for relative time; pinned `locale` / `timezoneId`; never set `workers: 1` globally — only via `npm run test:destructive` for `@destructive` tests.

```typescript
// ✅ Good — config retries already in playwright.config.ts; clock advised (no in-repo clock yet)
// retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0
await page.clock.install({ time: new Date("2026-08-04T18:00:00Z") });
// Serial destructive slice only:
// npm run test:destructive  →  playwright test --grep @destructive --workers=1
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
// retries: 5
// playwright.config.ts → workers: 1   // serializes the whole suite
const label = `Updated ${new Date().toLocaleString()}`; // wall-clock drift local vs CI
let sharedProgramName = ""; // mutable state across tests
```

**Why:** Prevents hidden flake, suite-wide serial bottlenecks, and date/time assertions that pass locally and fail in CI. *(Pin `locale` + `timezoneId` in config once chosen — not set yet.)*

